 ('frz8', 'The identification was confirmed when edicts were found, including at Maski, that use the name Ashoka directly. A king who had been almost forgotten in India was read back into history from his own stones.'),
 ('plq1', 'Devanampiya Piyadasi is the title the edicts mostly use; Ashoka is the name, confirmed by the inscriptions that carry it.'),
 ('plq2', 'If a question gives you Piyadasi, it is testing whether you know they are the same man.'),
 ('ins1', 'Here is the memory tip, carved in stone. Two surfaces: rocks and pillars. Two main scripts: Brahmi, and Kharoshthi in the north-west.'),
 ('quiz1', 'Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. First: the script, written right to left and developed from Aramaic, used for the north-western edicts. Kharoshthi.'),
 ('quiz2', 'Second: the edict site on the east coast, in the Kalinga country, carrying a special edict on governing conquered people. Dhauli — Jaugada also counts.'),
